xnu 0399: the HCI control bluetoothd's POSIX driver opens

0386 put the HCI transport behind /dev/cu.bluetooth on the strength of the
access() pair and the termios imports in bluetoothd. The disassembly of
/usr/sbin/bluetoothd (16.7.16) says otherwise: BT_HCI_TRANSPORT parses as
"transport:driver[:parameter]", and the POSIX driver takes the parameter as
the kernel control name it resolves with CTLIOCGINFO at 0x100066d34.

That name selects the data path. With exactly "com.apple.uart.sk.bluetooth"
the driver asks for a 16-byte UUID (0x40105412) and feeds it to
os_channel_create(); this kernel has no Skywalk (SKYWALK_BASE is not built), so
the call returns NULL and the driver closes the socket. It does not fall back -
measured 2026-11-20, errno ENOENT and "Failed to initialize driver POSIX". With
any other control name the same driver takes the plain path at 0x100066e50: it
sets the termios and the 12-byte UART option, leaves its channel pointer NULL,
and writes and reads the control socket directly (0x1000676a4, 0x100067a9c).

This registers the control as com.mondrian.btuart; the shim passes that name as
the third token, BT_HCI_TRANSPORT=H4:POSIX:com.mondrian.btuart. It forwards
write(2) to
the existing QUP wrapper 2 engine and polls the engine every 10 ms into
ctl_enqueuedata(), the same rate the tty used: 115 bytes at 115200 fit the
128-byte RX FIFO. The termios and UART options the daemon sets are accepted
and printed; the engine stays at the measured 14745600 Hz root and 8N1.

The control is registered from the root-mount probe block, next to
mondrian_bt_tty_init(), before launchd starts bluetoothd. /dev/cu.bluetooth
stays, because FindLocalMacAddress looks for it before the control is used.

On top of the transport, the control translates the LE scan the daemon asks
for. bluetoothd's init-time report sets enableExtendedScanHCI=0, so it drives
LE Set Scan Parameters and LE Set Scan Enable (0x200b/0x200c); the WCN7850
refuses that pair with 0x0c "command disallowed" in every state measured
2026-11-21 and delivers no report, which is why the device list stayed on
addresses. The extended pair (0x2041/0x2042) completes with status 0 and
produces LE meta events. The control rewrites the two commands, answers the
daemon with the completion for the opcode it sent, swallows the controller's
completion, and turns each extended advertising report (subevent 0x0d) into
the legacy report (subevent 0x02) bluetoothd parses.

Two details of the translation are measured. The own address type goes out as
public: bluetoothd asks for the random type, and the controller answers the
extended enable with 0x12 "invalid parameters" when no random address is set
(the shim probe: own=1 -> 18, own=0 -> 0, same interval, window and filter).
Each translated command gets its own pending count, not a flag: bluetoothd
sends an enable and a disable back to back, and one flag let the second
completion through to the daemon, which exits on an opcode it never sent
(measured 2026-11-21, the exit report named "Unknown" at hcievents.c).

The controller's byte stream needs the same care as its reports: it emits
0xFD/0xFE wake filler between packets, it answers the shim's 0xFD wake with a
bare 0xFC, and a completion the previous daemon never read stays in the FIFO
and reaches the next one as the answer to its own reset. The stack exits on an
unexpected completion and leaves its own behind, so that chain never ends by
itself (measured 2026-11-21: every run died processing OI_DHCI_RESET_OPCODE,
and one read carried two identical reset completions). The poll path skips the
filler, keeps the bare handshake byte, and reassembles whole packets; the
connect path drains the FIFO first.

HCI-IBS is the other half of that stream: 0xFD is the controller's WAKE_IND and
needs a 0xFC from the host, or the part repeats it every 100 ms and takes no
command (hci_qca.c). The shim answers while it boots the firmware; after it
closes the control nobody did, and a daemon whose reset followed a fresh NVM
download got 2 KB of 0xFD/0xFE and no packet (measured 2026-11-21). The poll
answers the indication now, under a write lock shared with the daemon's
commands so the ACK cannot interleave with a command.

