A legacy report carries at most 31 data bytes, and an extended advertisement
can be longer. Cutting the payload at 31 bytes left the last AD structure
promising more bytes than the event carried, and the daemon rejected the whole
report (`Invalid advertising response data size (1 + 66)`, measured 2026-11-21
on a 67-byte advertisement). The translation copies whole AD structures only
and drops the incomplete tail, so a report with a long service-data field
keeps its flags and name instead of disappearing.

The name in the classic inquiry EIR is not used by this bluetoothd for a
dual-mode device: it answers kCBMsgIdDeviceGetNameMsg with an empty name
(measured 2026-11-21, result 212, name length 0) while the EIR for the same
address says "55...", so the pane draws the address. The controller's scan
responses carry the shortened name type (0x08), and this daemon does not use
it either (`55" Crys` for C8:A6:EF, still an empty reply), so the copy
promotes it to Complete Local Name (0x09). The control also keeps the EIR name
and appends it as a Complete Local Name when the advertisement carries neither
form; trailing structures are dropped when the 31-byte legacy payload needs
the room. The controller sends legacy reports as well, so both paths share the
bridge.

The classic name request is one byte short. Remote Name Request (0x0419) takes
BD_ADDR, packet type, clock offset and the page-scan repetition mode, 11 bytes
in all; bluetoothd sends 10 and the controller rejects the command, so no
completion and no name arrive. The control appends the repetition mode (R0)
and forwards 11.

